Inheritance in Python allows a class (child/derived class) to reuse and extend the attributes and methods of another class (parent/base class). 
 Key Concepts of Inheritance in Python
•	Parent/Base Class: The class whose properties and methods are inherited.
•	Child/Derived Class: The class that inherits from the parent class.

Syntax: 
•	class Parent:
# parent class code
•	class Child(Parent):
# child class code
•	IS-A Relationship: Inheritance is used when one class is a specialized version of another.

Example: A Dog is a type of Animal.

Example
# Parent class
class Animal:
    def __init__(self, name):
        self.name = name

    def info(self):
        print("Animal name:", self.name)

# Child class
class Dog(Animal):
    def sound(self):
        print(self.name, "barks")

# Usage
d = Dog("Buddy")
d.info()   # Inherited method
d.sound()  # Child-specific method
Output:
Animal name: Buddy
Buddy barks
Here:
•	Dog inherits the info() method from Animal.
•	Dog adds its own method sound().

Types of Inheritance in Python

•	Single Inheritance: One child inherits from one parent.
•	Multiple Inheritance: A child inherits from multiple parents.
•	Multilevel Inheritance: A chain of inheritance (e.g., Grandparent → Parent → Child).
•	Hierarchical Inheritance: Multiple children inherit from the same parent.
•	Hybrid Inheritance: A combination of the above types.

Benefits of Inheritance
•	Code Reusability: Avoids rewriting common functionality.
•	Extensibility: Child classes can override or extend parent methods.
•	Organized Structure: Models real-world relationships (e.g., Vehicle → Car, Bike).


In Python, there are five main types of inheritance: Single, Multiple, Multilevel, Hierarchical, and Hybrid. Each type describes how classes relate to one another and how properties/methods are passed down.
Types of Inheritance in Python
1. Single Inheritance
•	Definition: A child class inherits from one parent class.
•	Example: 
	class Parent:
	    def func1(self):
	        print("This is parent class.")
	
	class Child(Parent):
	    def func2(self):
	        print("This is child class.")
	
	obj = Child()
	obj.func1()
	obj.func2()
	Use case: Simple extension of functionality.

2. Multiple Inheritance
	Definition: A child class inherits from more than one parent class.
•	Example: 
	class Father:
	    def skills(self):
	        print("Father: Driving")
	
	class Mother:
	    def skills(self):
	        print("Mother: Cooking")
	
	class Child(Father, Mother):
	    pass
	
	obj = Child()
	obj.skills()  # Resolves using Method Resolution Order (MRO)
	Use case: Combining features from multiple sources.

3. Multilevel Inheritance
•	Definition: A chain of inheritance (grandparent → parent → child).
	Example: 
	class Grandparent:
	    def func1(self):
	        print("Grandparent class")
	
	class Parent(Grandparent):
	    def func2(self):
	        print("Parent class")
	
	class Child(Parent):
	    def func3(self):
	        print("Child class")
	
obj = Child()
obj.func1()
obj.func2()
obj.func3()
Use case: Step-by-step specialization.

4. Hierarchical Inheritance
Definition: Multiple child classes inherit from the same parent.
•	Example: 
	class Parent:
	    def func(self):
	        print("Parent class")

class Child1(Parent):
	    pass
	
class Child2(Parent):
	    pass
	
obj1 = Child1()
obj2 = Child2()
obj1.func()
obj2.func()
Use case: Shared functionality across different subclasses.

5. Hybrid Inheritance
•	Definition: A combination of two or more types of inheritance.
•	Example: A mix of multiple and hierarchical inheritance.
•	Note: Can get complex; Python uses MRO (Method Resolution Order) to resolve conflicts.
Key Takeaways
Single → One parent, one child.
Multiple → One child, many parents.
Multilevel → Chain of inheritance.
Hierarchical → One parent, many children.
Hybrid → Combination of the above.



